{"id": "esr", "title": "ESR", "questions": [{"badge": "JEE Main 2026 - 05 Apr S1", "q": "For the Friedel-Crafts alkylation of benzene with 1-chloropropane and anhydrous AlCl3, which statements are correct? A. Major product is n-propylbenzene. B. Isopropyl carbocation intermediate is also generated. C. Multiple substitution is inevitable. D. Introducing an electron-donating substituent on benzene will not produce any alkylbenzene.", "options": ["A and D only", "B and C only", "A and C only", "B and D only"], "answer": 1, "explain": "The 1° carbocation rearranges to isopropyl, so the major product is isopropylbenzene; the alkyl product is more reactive, so polysubstitution happens.", "img": "data:image/jpeg;base64,"}, {"badge": "JEE Main 2026 - 28 Jan S2", "q": "Identify the correct statements. The presence of a -NO2 group in a benzene ring: A. activates the ring towards electrophilic substitutions. B. deactivates the ring towards electrophilic substitutions. C. activates the ring towards nucleophilic substitutions. D. deactivates the ring towards nucleophilic substitutions.", "options": ["C and A only", "B and C only", "A and D only", "B and D only"], "answer": 1, "explain": "NO2 withdraws by -M and -I: deactivates EAS, makes nucleophilic substitution easier."}, {"badge": "JEE Main 2021 - 20 Jul S2", "q": "Benzene on nitration gives nitrobenzene in the presence of HNO3 and H2SO4 mixture, where:", "options": ["both H2SO4 and HNO3 act as bases", "HNO3 acts as an acid and H2SO4 acts as a base", "both H2SO4 and HNO3 act as acids", "HNO3 acts as a base and H2SO4 acts as an acid"], "answer": 3, "explain": "H2SO4 protonates HNO3, which loses water to form NO2+; the proton acceptor (HNO3) is the base here."}, {"badge": "JEE Main 2021 - 25 Feb S2", "q": "The correct sequence of reagents used in the preparation of 4-bromo-2-nitroethylbenzene from benzene is:", "options": ["CH3COCl/AlCl3, Br2/AlBr3, HNO3/H2SO4, Zn/HCl", "CH3COCl/AlCl3, Zn-Hg/HCl, Br2/AlBr3, HNO3/H2SO4", "HNO3/H2SO4, Br2/AlCl3, CH3COCl/AlCl3, Zn-Hg/HCl", "Br2/AlBr3, CH3COCl/AlCl3, HNO3/H2SO4, Zn/HCl"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Acylate first (no rearrangement), Clemmensen-reduce to ethyl (o/p director), brominate para, then nitration goes ortho to ethyl."}, {"badge": "JEE Main 2019 - 08 Apr S2", "q": "Polysubstitution is a major drawback in:", "options": ["Reimer-Tiemann reaction", "Friedel-Crafts alkylation", "Acetylation of aniline", "Friedel-Crafts acylation"], "answer": 1, "explain": "The alkyl product is more reactive than benzene, so it keeps reacting; acylation products are deactivated, so it stops at one."}, {"badge": "JEE Main 2019 - 09 Apr S1", "q": "The increasing order of reactivity of the following compounds towards aromatic electrophilic substitution reaction is: A = chlorobenzene, B = anisole, C = toluene, D = benzonitrile", "options": ["A<B<C<D", "B<C<A<D", "D<B<A<C", "D<A<C<B"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Benzonitrile is most deactivated (-M), chlorobenzene deactivating but o/p, toluene activates mildly, anisole activates strongly."}, {"badge": "JEE Main 2021 - 24 Feb S2", "q": "What is the correct sequence of reagents used for converting nitrobenzene into m-dibromobenzene?", "options": ["Sn/HCl -> KBr -> Br2 -> H+", "Br2/Fe -> Sn/HCl -> NaNO2/HCl -> CuBr/HBr", "NaNO2 -> HCl -> KBr -> H+", "Sn/HCl -> Br2 -> NaNO2 -> NaBr"], "answer": 1, "explain": "NO2 is meta-directing, so brominate first; then reduce to aniline, diazotise, and Sandmeyer replaces N2+ with Br at that same position."}]}
